Resolution and verification no longer overflow the stack when they ask whether a recursive datatype with no values is nonempty, as in `datatype D = K(t: D)` passed for a type parameter declared `(00)`, a type declared `(00)` refined by such a datatype, or a local variable of such a type. The question is answered by following the grounding constructor of each datatype. A datatype with no values has none, so the answer followed a constructor that leads back to the datatype: directly, through another datatype or a total arrow, or through a type that grows at each step, as in `datatype P<X> = P(p: P<seq<X>>)`. Such a type is now reported as possibly empty, with the usual error where a nonempty type is required. Every type whose answer was found before keeps it (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/413).
